# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  CS193V container flags.  One flag (or flag + value) per line; # starts a comment.
#  Read by ./cs193v — see `cs193v --dev-print-command` for the exact resulting line.
#
#  Machine-specific values (the memory cap) live in local.args, which the installer
#  writes and git ignores.
#
#  EVERY LINE HERE IS A PODMAN FLAG. There is no line that names the image, and adding one
#  would not work: the launcher always runs what .private/Containerfile builds on the
#  student's own machine. There is no registry to point at — see README.md's "How the image
#  reaches a student" for why that was decided, and reversed, and decided again.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  INVARIANTS — never add any of these
#
#    --privileged            --cap-add=SYS_ADMIN      --cap-add=SYS_PTRACE
#    --network=host          --pid=host               --ipc=host
#    --userns=host           --security-opt seccomp=unconfined
#    --security-opt unmask=ALL
#    ...and never mount a podman or docker socket.
#
#  THE TUNNEL'S OWN INVARIANTS. The host runs the ssh CLIENT and the container runs sshd;
#  every one of these keeps it that way round, and the direction is the entire security
#  argument -- see files/sshd_config.
#
#    never  ssh -R  (or RemoteForward, or `ssh -O forward -R`)
#        A remote forward is the mirror image of what the tunnel does: the CONTAINER would
#        ask the HOST to open a listening port. That is the "container tells your computer
#        which ports to open" channel this project exists to avoid, it would need an sshd
#        running on every student's laptop, and it would need a credential inside the
#        container that logs in to the host. Refused by the server too (AllowTcpForwarding
#        local), so this line is documentation of a property, not the only thing enforcing it.
#
#    never  -o ForwardAgent=yes / AddKeysToAgent / ssh-agent forwarding of any kind
#        CONTAINER-DESIGN.md promises a student's own ssh and gpg keys never enter the
#        container, and agent forwarding would hand it live use of them.
#
#    never  -o ClearAllForwardings=yes
#        Not a security rule, a correctness one, and it reads like the opposite of what it
#        does: ssh_config(5) clears forwardings given on the COMMAND LINE too, so it would
#        silently delete all 46 forwards. Use `-F none` to ignore the student's own config.
#
#    never  ssh without -N
#        The tunnel needs no remote command, and asking for one would allocate a shell and a
#        pty inside the container for no reason.
#
#  Host isolation comes from rootless podman's user namespace. It does NOT come from
#  the capability set: root owns /usr/bin and /etc, so euid 0 inside the container can
#  tamper with the toolchain using no capability at all. Nor from AppArmor, which is
#  not confining this container — /proc/self/attr/current reads "crun (unconfined)".
#
#  Also do NOT copy the devcontainer CLI's automatic --security-opt label=disable. It
#  silently strips SELinux type enforcement for anyone on Fedora or RHEL. The launcher
#  adds `:z` to the workspace mount alone when SELinux is enforcing.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ─── Identity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The hostname a student sees in their prompt. Ubuntu's default PS1 is \u@\h:\w$, so this
# single flag makes every prompt line read student@cs193v-development -- the cheapest
# possible "you are somewhere else" signal, present on every line, surviving nano, costing
# nothing. Without it the prompt shows a random hex container id, which tells a student
# nothing at all. See issue #3.
--hostname cs193v-development

# The EXPLICIT uid=/gid= form, not bare --userns=keep-id. The host side is read from
# getuid() at launch, so this always means "whoever ran this command" -> container
# 1000. Bare keep-id would map the host uid to the SAME number, which breaks on macOS
# (501) and on six-digit enterprise uids. Rootless podman structurally cannot map
# another account's uid: newuidmap checks /etc/subuid.
#
# NOTE: --userns=keep-id is a HARD ERROR on a rootful connection, not a no-op. The
# launcher preflights for rootless and explains, rather than emitting a raw error.
--userns=keep-id:uid=1000,gid=1000
--user 1000:1000

# ─── Networking ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Explicit, even though pasta is the rootless default from podman 5.0 — this is immune
# to a stale ~/.config/containers/containers.conf naming slirp4netns or a bridge.
--network=pasta

# DO NOT ADD  --network=pasta:--host-lo-to-ns-lo
#   It is a real flag and it works on native Linux and WSL2 (verified in Ubuntu
#   26.04's passt snapshot: fwd.c rewrites the destination to loopback, and it is not
#   deprecated). But it is SILENTLY INERT on macOS, because podman inside the machine
#   VM strips HostIP and gvproxy dials 192.168.127.2, so the splice path is never
#   taken. Shipping it would make identical student code work on Ubuntu and Windows
#   and fail on Macs, which is the worst kind of divergence.
#
#   SUPERSEDED, not wrong: the launcher now solves the same problem one layer up, with an
#   ssh tunnel from the host into the container's own loopback. That deliberately does not
#   touch the network stack at all -- its transport is a `podman exec` pipe, which is the
#   thing this whole launcher already relies on to open a shell on all three platforms.
#   The failure above is the reason it is done that way: any fix that rides the pasta or
#   gvproxy path has to be proven separately on every platform, and this one has no
#   network path to diverge. See "Published ports" below.
#
# DO NOT ADD  --network=pasta:-t,auto   (or any -t <addr>/auto form)
#   Three independent failures on the passt Ubuntu 26.04 ships: `127.0.0.1/auto` does
#   not parse (that grammar landed upstream three months after Ubuntu's snapshot);
#   `-t auto` together with `-p` is FATAL, and podman appends its -p-derived -t after
#   user options so the container never starts; and the bare form registers a NULL bind
#   address across ports 1-65535, publishing every listening container port — including
#   Claude Code's own sockets — to the local network, with no way to pin it to loopback.

# ─── Published ports: there are none, deliberately ─────────────────────────────
# There are no -p lines here any more, and adding one back would BREAK the tunnel rather
# than supplement it.
#
# `podman run -p 127.0.0.1:3000:3000` and `ssh -L 127.0.0.1:3000:...` both want to bind host
# 127.0.0.1:3000, and the second one to try gets EADDRINUSE. They are two implementations of
# the same thing, not belt and braces, so the tunnel REPLACES publishing port for port.
#
# Why the tunnel won: podman's forwarder delivers to the container's eth0 and never touches
# its lo, so a server bound to the container's 127.0.0.1 was unreachable from the student's
# browser -- while its own log cheerfully printed "Local: http://localhost:5173/". Ten weeks
# of every student hitting that, and `--host 0.0.0.0` on every command, was the cost of this
# section. The tunnel's far end is resolved INSIDE the container, so it reaches lo; and since
# 0.0.0.0 includes lo, it also reaches everything -p used to. It is a strict superset.
#
# Two properties that came free, and one that did not:
#
#   * Ports are no longer frozen at container-create time. podman cannot add a mapping to a
#     running container, but `ssh -O forward` can add one to a running TUNNEL -- so a port
#     could be opened on demand with no recreate.
#   * The host side is still loopback-only, and now for a structural reason rather than a
#     flag: the ssh client binds 127.0.0.1 itself. Nothing is exposed to dorm wifi.
#   * NOT free: a host port that is already busy. `podman run` used to fail loudly (and
#     take the whole container down with it); the launcher now probes each port first and
#     reports the ones it could not take. See ensure_tunnel in the launcher.
#
# The port LIST itself lives in CS193V_PORTS below -- one declaration, used both to build the
# forwards and to tell the container which ones they are, so the two cannot disagree.

# ─── Persistent state ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Five volumes holding logins and settings, and ONE holding a package cache. That last one is
# an exception to a rule this file used to state flatly, so here is the rule and why the
# exception earns it.
#
# The rule: no package caches. node_modules lives on the bind mount and survives a rebuild
# anyway, so caching it would not make --rebuild cheap — and a persisted cache would survive
# the rebuild meant to clear it.
#
# Why ms-playwright is different on both counts. It does NOT live on the bind mount: it is
# under $HOME, so a --rebuild throws it away with the rest of the writable layer. And what
# gets thrown away is a 114 MB browser download, not a few seconds of npm work. The image
# ships one Chromium build already, so the volume is not what makes the common case work —
# it is what stops a student who needs a DIFFERENT playwright version from re-downloading
# its browser after every rebuild. --rebuild --logout still clears it, and the image's own copy
# comes straight back, so nothing is stranded.
#
# The mount targets are pre-created student-owned in the Containerfile. That is what
# makes podman's auto-chown work and lets the entrypoint run with no root phase. For
# ms-playwright it decides one more thing: podman copies the image's content into an EMPTY
# named volume on first mount, so the ownership baked into the image is the ownership the
# volume inherits.
-v cs193v-claude:/home/student/.claude
-v cs193v-claude-json:/home/student/.claude-json
-v cs193v-gh:/home/student/.config/gh
# The git identity and the gh credential helper line, which is why this one exists and why it is
# .config/git rather than ~/.gitconfig. `git config --global` writes $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/config
# when that file exists and ~/.gitconfig does not, so the Containerfile creates it empty and
# every --global write lands in here. Without the volume they land in the writable layer, and
# --rebuild -- the command staff tell students to run to GET a fix -- would silently take away
# the identity setup-git configured and the credential helper `gh auth setup-git` wrote, while
# the token itself survived next door in cs193v-gh. The student's next `git commit` would then
# ask them who they are, for a reason nothing on their screen could explain.
-v cs193v-git:/home/student/.config/git
-v cs193v-vercel:/home/student/.local/share/com.vercel.cli
-v cs193v-playwright:/home/student/.cache/ms-playwright

# ─── Environment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE port list, and the only one. The launcher parses this line to build its ssh -L
# forwards, and passes the same value into the container so a student -- or the agent
# answering their question -- can see which ports reach their browser.
#
# It used to have to stay in step with a parallel set of -p lines, and tests/10-static.sh
# asserted that agreement, because a list that disagreed with the forwards gives confidently
# wrong advice -- worse than no advice. Deriving the forwards from this line instead makes that
# drift STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE, so the test was deleted rather than rewritten. The remaining
# drift risk is the ranges quoted in the managed CLAUDE.md, which is still checked at edit time.
#
# All >= 1024 (rootless podman refused privileged host ports, and an unprivileged ssh client
# cannot bind them either) and avoiding 5000 and 7000, which macOS AirPlay Receiver occupies.
#
# Taught as "the 173 family": 4173 preview, 5173 vite, 6173 spare.
#   3000-3009  next, express, bare node, serve, docusaurus
#   4173-4176  vite preview
#   5173-5179  vite dev -- the range vite's auto-increment lands in
#   6173-6182  SPARE / assign-on-demand
#   8000-8009  python http.server, django, uvicorn
#   8080-8084  webpack-dev-server, http-server, misc
#
# Still hashed into cs193v.confighash, so changing it still prompts a recreate. It NEED not
# be -- forwards are negotiated per session now -- but this value reaches the container
# through -e at create time, so decoupling them would leave the container naming a set that is
# no longer forwarded. A deliberate non-goal, not an oversight.
-e CS193V_PORTS=3000-3009,4173-4176,5173-5179,6173-6182,8000-8009,8080-8084

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Deliberately ABSENT — each of these was considered and rejected
#
#  --cap-drop=ALL / --security-opt=no-new-privileges
#      Mutually exclusive with sudo by construction: /usr/bin/sudo is setuid, and
#      no-new-privileges makes execve ignore setuid bits. They would also buy nothing,
#      since root owns the tamper targets and needs no capability to modify them.
#
#  --memory-swap
#      The "equal to --memory" idiom is DOCKER's semantics. podman-run(1) requires the
#      swap value to be strictly larger, so shipping it may make the container refuse
#      to start — every student seeing an opaque failure on the flag meant to help.
#
#  --pids-limit (tighter than podman's 2048 default)
#      Exhaustion does not kill the container, it WEDGES it: `podman exec` must fork
#      into the same cgroup, so the launcher cannot get back in. It does not self-heal.
#
#  --cpus
#      The cpu controller is frequently not delegated to rootless users, so this is
#      silently ignored or errors depending on the distro.
#
#  --tmpfs /tmp
#      The devcontainer's --tmpfs runArg was a workaround for buildah#6503 during
#      FEATURE installs. There are no features here. /tmp on the writable layer means
#      no RAM pressure and no size to tune, and --rebuild clears it anyway.
#
#  --init
#      Bind-mounts the HOST's catatonit, which Ubuntu's podman package only Recommends
#      — so a host missing it fails to start the container at all. PID 1 is a shell
#      keep-alive loop in the image instead; see files/entrypoint.sh.
#
#  --shm-size
#      A Chromium IS installed now, so the old reason for this line ("only mattered for
#      Chrome, which is not installed") is gone — but the conclusion survives, for a better
#      reason. Playwright puts --disable-dev-shm-usage in its own default chromium arguments,
#      so the 64 MB /dev/shm that would otherwise crash a browser on a heavy page is never
#      used as shared memory in the first place. Verified in playwright-core 1.62.1's default
#      argument list, not inferred from the docs. Raising it would buy nothing measurable and
#      would cost RAM on every student's machine.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
